# =========================================================================== #
# What must not go to GitHub, and why.
#
# The rule of thumb for this repo: commit anything that was *written*, ignore
# anything that was *downloaded, generated, or measured on this machine*. The
# question bank is written, so it is committed — it is most of the value here.
# =========================================================================== #


# --- Would be rejected by GitHub outright --------------------------------- #

# The Piper voice is a 113.9 MB ONNX file. GitHub hard-rejects any file over
# 100 MB, so pushing this does not merely bloat the repo, it fails the push.
# Re-download it from `rhasspy/piper-voices` on HuggingFace:
#   en_US-lessac-high.onnx  ->  data/voices/
data/voices/*.onnx

# The matching config is 5 KB and the app needs it, so it stays. Piper reads
# the sample rate and phoneme map from here, and it is not derivable.
!data/voices/*.onnx.json


# --- Big, and reproducible from a lockfile -------------------------------- #

# 2.8 GB, and machine-specific: the CUDA wheels inside it are pinned to this
# box's driver. `uv sync` rebuilds it from pyproject.toml + uv.lock.
.venv/
venv/
env/


# --- Personal data -------------------------------------------------------- #

# Every interview ever taken: the questions, the transcribed answers, the
# verdicts, and the scores. This is a record of what its owner did and did not
# know, which is nobody else's business. It is also recreated empty on first
# run, so nothing breaks by leaving it out.
data/sessions.db
data/*.db-journal
data/*.db-wal


# --- Generated or measured on this machine -------------------------------- #

# Per-phase verification output. Real numbers, but they describe this GTX
# 1650 Ti on this evening — committing them invites someone to read a stale
# latency figure as a promise. Regenerate by running the harness. Nothing in
# bench/ is hand-written JSON, so the whole extension goes.
bench/*.json

# Scratch files the harnesses drop as they run: server and Ollama logs, and the
# synthesized answer audio the Phase 1 and Phase 5 tests speak into the socket.
# Those two WAVs alone were 1 MB, more than half of everything else combined.
# They all use a leading underscore, which is what makes one rule enough.
bench/_*
*.log

# Backup of the generated Python bank, kept only while the hand-written
# replacement was being written. The real one is data/bank/python.json.
*.bak
*.orig


# --- Python ---------------------------------------------------------------- #

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/


# --- Editors and OS -------------------------------------------------------- #

.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store


# --- Secrets ---------------------------------------------------------------- #

# Nothing here needs a key today — the whole point of this project is that it
# runs locally with no API. The rule stays anyway, so that the day something
# does need one, it is not one `git add .` away from being public.
.env
.env.*
*.key
*.pem


# =========================================================================== #
# Deliberately NOT ignored, so the repo is useful to clone:
#
#   data/bank/*.json      the question bank — 197 questions, and the 66 Python
#                         ones are hand-written. This is the project's content,
#                         not a build artifact. 308 KB total.
#   uv.lock               pins the exact dependency set, including the CPU-only
#                         torch index. Without it a fresh `uv sync` pulls the
#                         CUDA torch build and wastes 2 GB on a package that is
#                         only there for Silero VAD.
#   web/                  hand-written; there is no build step and no bundler.
# =========================================================================== #
